The gap this paper addresses is that the two mature families of systems sit at opposite ends of this
ladder and neither spans it. Federated social systems --- ActivityPub~\cite{activitypub},
Matrix~\cite{matrixspec}, the AT Protocol~\cite{atproto} --- assume L0 and degrade to unavailability
at L1, because federation is defined in terms of reaching a named remote host. Delay-tolerant and
mesh systems --- DTN~\cite{fall2003dtn,rfc9171}, Serval~\cite{gardnerstephen2011serval},
Bridgefy~\cite{albrecht2021bridgefy} --- begin at L4 and provide no content, identity or moderation
model of the kind a public forum needs. A population under a shutdown needs the middle of the ladder,
which is where neither family operates.

We are explicit at the outset that the \emph{mechanisms} we use are not new. Content addressed by the
hash of its canonical encoding, signed at rest, is Nostr~\cite{nostr} and Secure
Scuttlebutt~\cite{tarr2019ssb}. Gossiped tree heads for detecting log rewriting is Certificate
Transparency~\cite{rfc6962}. Separating credential issuance from credential verification is Privacy
Pass~\cite{davidson2018privacypass}. Preferring the narrowest working path and keeping alternates warm
is ICE~\cite{rfc8445} and MPTCP~\cite{rfc8684}. Our contribution is the composition, the invariants
that composition forces, and what it costs when measured.

\noindent This paper contributes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Canonicalisation as an \emph{admission} predicate, and the three-position design
    space it completes.} Prior systems either canonicalise inside the verifier (Matrix) or refuse to
    define a canonical form at all (Cosmos SDK). We show these are two of three positions, take the
    third, and give the property it buys: no canonicalisation function is ever interposed between an
    attacker and a verifier, so a re-encoding relay cannot silently \emph{repair} a malformed object
    into a verifiable one (\S\ref{sec:canonical}).
  \item \textbf{Fork detection that stays safe when a consistency proof cannot be fetched.} The CT
    gossip literature resolves ambiguous tree-head pairs with a consistency proof. That proof is a
    round trip to the peer whose reachability is in doubt --- unavailable exactly during a partition,
    which is when fork detection is most likely to fire. We show the resulting false accusations are
    real and severe (one uplink flap falsely blocked two of three honest peers), give a strictly
    weaker rule that needs only a field the head already carries and no round trip, and show why the
    obvious implementation of it reintroduces the bug (\S\ref{sec:fork}).
  \item \textbf{Two federation invariants, each stated with the deployed failure that produced it}:
    an identifier inside a federated payload must be derivable by any node from the signed bytes, and
    admission cost must be charged at the origin because node-local anti-abuse secrets are meaningless
    elsewhere (\S\ref{sec:federation}).
  \item \textbf{A partition testbed, a resilience ladder that makes such systems comparable, and the
    first measurements of this one} --- propagation swept over node count and queueing parameter,
    bridged crossing under a severed exchange, resident memory against a single-board-computer budget,
    encoded size against a measured ActivityPub baseline, and a denial-of-service surface measured
    rather than asserted (\S\ref{sec:eval}).
\end{itemize}

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

Because this venue spans networking and security broadly, we introduce each concept rather than
assuming it.

\paragraph{Delay-tolerant networking.} DTN~\cite{fall2003dtn} and Bundle Protocol
v7~\cite{rfc9171} are the architectural ancestor of anything that must survive a partition: bundles
are stored and forwarded over whatever bearer exists, with no assumption of an end-to-end path. BPv7
is the one prior system that covers our entire ladder, and we say so plainly in
\S\ref{sec:comparison}. What it does not supply is a content model, an identity model, or moderation.
It is also weaker on integrity than is commonly assumed: BPv7 delegates it to BPSec~\cite{rfc9172},
whose use per bundle is optional and whose only default context is HMAC-SHA2 with symmetric
keys~\cite{rfc9173} --- so a default-context integrity block is not third-party verifiable.

\paragraph{Mesh messaging.} Serval's Rhizome~\cite{gardnerstephen2011serval} distributes signed
bundles over ad-hoc links with no infrastructure whatsoever, and remains the strongest prior art for
verification without a server: the bundle identifier \emph{is} the verification key. Bridgefy carries
Bluetooth mesh messaging and was broken comprehensively in 2020~\cite{albrecht2021bridgefy}; its
subsequent adoption of the Signal protocol fixed confidentiality but, by design, provides deniability
rather than third-party verifiability~\cite{signalx3dh}, and broadcast messages remained
unauthenticated in follow-up analysis~\cite{albrecht2022collective}.

\paragraph{Federation over IP.} ActivityPub~\cite{activitypub} is the dominant federation protocol.
Notably, it makes neither object signatures nor HTTP Signatures normative --- its authentication
discussion is confined to a non-normative appendix which concedes that no strongly agreed mechanism
existed at standardisation time. Interoperable authentication in deployed ActivityPub is therefore
convention, and origin-validation defects follow from that~\cite{cve2024mastodon}.
Matrix~\cite{matrixspec} does sign events, over a Canonical JSON form. The AT
Protocol~\cite{atproto} is the closest prior composition to ours: signed commits over a Merkle search
tree, with deterministic CBOR encoding. It differs from us on two axes we care about, discussed in
\S\ref{sec:comparison}.

\paragraph{Transparency logs.} Certificate Transparency~\cite{rfc6962} established the pattern we
reuse: an append-only Merkle log~\cite{merkle1988} whose signed tree heads are gossiped, so that a
participant which rewrites history is detectable by anyone comparing two heads. How to compare heads
efficiently and safely is itself well studied --- gossip protocols for verifying log
consistency~\cite{chuat2015gossip} and an aggregation-based design that discards a smaller head once
one tree provably extends the other~\cite{dahlberg2018aggregation}. Both resolutions rest on obtaining
a consistency proof; \S\ref{sec:fork} is about what to do in the window where that proof cannot be
fetched, which in a partitioned network is not a rare case.

\paragraph{Path selection.} ICE~\cite{rfc8445} and MPTCP~\cite{rfc8684} establish that maintaining
several candidate paths and continuously preferring among them is more robust than failing over on
demand. We apply the same reasoning to reachability \emph{scope} rather than to addresses.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Design}
\label{sec:design}

\subsection{One write path}

The system exposes exactly one write endpoint. A post, a vote, a moderation action, a key revocation
and a broadcast are all the same structure: an \emph{envelope} carrying a domain string, an author
key, a timestamp, a priority class, an opaque body, and a signature. There is no per-feature write
route, and adding a feature adds a registry row and a handler rather than a branch anywhere in the
ingress path.

The envelope's identifier is derived from its own bytes:
\[
\textit{id} = \textsc{base32}\big(\textsc{sha-256}(\textit{canonical}(\textit{fields}_{1..12}))\big)
\]
and the Ed25519~\cite{bernstein2012ed25519} signature covers those same bytes. Nothing about this
identifier depends on which node computed it, which is what allows two independent instances to agree
on identity without coordinating.

\subsection{Canonical encoding as an admission gate}
\label{sec:canonical}

Canonical encoding is where our design differs most sharply from prior work, and the difference is
not \emph{whether} to canonicalise but \emph{where in the pipeline} canonicalisation sits and what
happens when it fails. Three positions exist in the literature.

Matrix canonicalises \emph{at verification time}: the receiver strips signature fields, re-encodes
the parsed object into Canonical JSON, and checks the signature against the result~\cite{matrixspec}.
The verifier therefore checks a signature against bytes it produced itself, and an intermediary may
re-serialise an event freely without invalidating it.

Cosmos SDK rejects canonicalisation outright: verification compares ``the raw \texttt{TxBody} and
\texttt{AuthInfo} bytes \ldots not based on any `canonicalization' algorithm'', which it argues adds
client complexity and prevents ``some forms of upgradeability''~\cite{cosmosadr020}. Whatever bytes
the sender emitted are the contract; there is nothing to reject.

We take the verification rule of the second and the insistence on a defined canonical form of the
first, and order them differently. The signature is checked against \textbf{the bytes as they
arrived}. Separately, and \emph{before} verification, the decoder re-encodes the parsed message and
compares; a mismatch is \textbf{rejected}, never repaired. Canonicalisation is thus not in the
verification path at all --- it is an admission predicate:

\begin{quote}
Matrix canonicalises to decide \emph{what to verify against}; Cosmos declines to canonicalise at all;
we canonicalise to decide \emph{what to admit}, and verify against what arrived.
\end{quote}

The hazard this forecloses is concrete. Under canonicalise-at-verify, a re-encoding intermediary
silently \emph{repairs} a malformed object into a verifiable one; a prior version of this system was
vulnerable to exactly that signature-confusion pattern. Under the Cosmos rule the hazard cannot arise
because the question is never asked. Under ours it cannot arise because the answer is rejection.

Protobuf serialisation ``is not (and cannot be) canonical''~\cite{protobufnotcanonical}, and that
guidance warns against byte-equality as a proxy for \emph{message equality}, where differing bytes for
one message give false negatives. We ask a narrower question --- \emph{is this the one accepted
encoding?} --- for which a second valid form is not an inconvenience but precisely the thing to
reject. Protobuf declining to define a canonical form is why a profile must define one and prove it
across implementations (\S\ref{sec:eval-vectors}).

We concede the cost that ADR-020 names: strictness impedes some forms of upgradeability. Unknown
fields are not retained, so an old node cannot forward-carry a field it does not understand; the
response is a version bump rather than an in-place contract edit.

\subsection{The validation pipeline}

Every envelope, from every transport, runs an identical nineteen-step pipeline. Two properties of
its ordering carry most of the weight.

\textbf{Steps 1--12 perform no database writes.} Everything through dedupe and replay completes
before anything is persisted, so an invalid-envelope flood consumes CPU but cannot amplify into write
load (\S\ref{sec:flood}).

\textbf{Steps 16 and 17 are atomic with respect to each other.} Projection and the Merkle append
occur in one transaction: an envelope visible in a projection but absent from the log is exactly the
transparency failure the log exists to prevent. The storage layer must therefore support
multi-document transactions --- a deployment constraint, not an implementation detail. Each step is
an independently testable pure function.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Federating Across a Partition}
\label{sec:federation}

Federation is server-to-server only. Clients do not speak it, because operators choose their peers
and clients do not, and because the protocol's connection profile degrades badly on lossy mobile
links.

\subsection{Four rules that carry the weight}

\paragraph{A peer's bytes are never re-encoded.} The transport uses a passthrough codec. Since the
decoder establishes canonicality by re-encoding and comparing (\S\ref{sec:canonical}), a round trip
through a generated codec in the network adapter would silently repair an untrusted peer's
non-canonical envelope into one that validates --- the exact hazard the admission gate exists to
foreclose, arriving over the network and bypassing it.

\paragraph{Trust bounds volume, never validity.} Inbound envelopes re-run all nineteen steps. A
trusted peer's forged envelope is rejected exactly as a stranger's is. Trust determines quota and
which priority classes a peer may push, and nothing else.

\paragraph{Deduplication is a unique index, not a read-then-write.} $(\textit{content\_id},
\textit{direction})$ is a uniqueness constraint in the database; a read-then-write check is unsound
under the concurrency federation produces, where an overlapping stream and a backfill can deliver one
envelope twice at once.

\paragraph{An envelope is never relayed to its sender.} The pipeline carries the origin peer and
excludes it from fanout. Content dedupe makes such a loop terminate but not free: without this, every
envelope pays a round trip before being discarded.

\subsection{Two invariants, and the failures that produced them}

\paragraph{An identifier in a federated payload must be derivable from the signed bytes.} An earlier
version derived a community identifier from the projecting node's own key. With one node this is
indistinguishable from correct, because the projecting node \emph{is} the origin node. With two, the
same signed envelope produced different community identifiers on each instance and the projections
diverged permanently. The rule that replaced it is a question asked before any identifier is minted:
\emph{would a different node, given only these bytes, compute the same value?}

\paragraph{Admission cost is charged at the origin.} Proof-of-work, credit balances, blind credentials
and epoch nullifiers are each keyed to one node deliberately. A proof minted at the origin is
therefore not merely unverifiable elsewhere --- it is meaningless elsewhere. Charging again on receipt
makes every anti-abuse-gated domain unfederatable. Cost is paid once, at origin; the receiver's
protection is a per-peer, per-class quota with an explicit backpressure hint. Verification is
unaffected: what is skipped is a payment, not a check.

A related consequence is that a peer exceeding quota is not disconnected. It would reconnect
immediately, pay the handshake again, and arrive in the same state, so refusing costs more than
accepting. The hint lets a well-behaved peer self-regulate, and a peer that ignores it is demoted.

\subsection{Federating from a node nothing can dial}
\label{sec:cgnat}

Server-to-server delivery in ActivityPub, Matrix and the AT Protocol is a \emph{push to an address
the receiver publishes}, so a node with no inbound reachability cannot receive at all unless a third
party holds an address for it. In the deployments this paper is about that is the common case, not
the corner case: a community node is a machine in somebody's home, behind carrier-grade NAT. We make
reachability an asymmetry rather than a requirement --- a node may advertise \emph{no} endpoints and
still federate both ways:

\begin{itemize}\itemsep1pt
  \item \textbf{outbound} is the durable outbox draining over a connection the node opened;
  \item \textbf{inbound} is a resumable backfill the node \emph{requests}, also over a connection it
    opened --- so nothing is pushed to it and nothing needs to reach it.
\end{itemize}

The property is asserted rather than argued: an unreachable node is introduced to a peer, the peer
records it with an empty endpoint list and can never dial it, and the test then shows a post
published on the unreachable node arriving at the peer, and a post published on the peer arriving at
the unreachable node. Neither direction is initiated by the reachable side.

\subsection{Fork detection when a consistency proof is unavailable}
\label{sec:fork}

A transparency log lets a participant detect a peer that rewrote history: tree heads are signed and
gossiped, and a head smaller than one already seen suggests entries were removed. This is the
Certificate Transparency construction~\cite{rfc6962} and we adopt it unchanged.

\paragraph{What the gossip literature already settles.} Comparing tree heads pairwise is known to be
subtle, and the CT gossip protocols address it directly: an STH-only gossip design observes that when
one tree provably \emph{extends} another, the smaller head can be discarded without reducing the
chance of detecting an attack~\cite{dahlberg2018aggregation}, and efficient gossip protocols for
exactly this consistency check are well studied~\cite{chuat2015gossip}. The resolution in that
literature is a \textbf{consistency proof} between the two heads.

\paragraph{What a partition changes.} A consistency proof must be fetched from the log. That is a
round trip to the very peer whose reachability is in question --- and in our setting the peer may be
behind the cut that is being routed around, which is precisely when fork detection is most likely to
fire. So the standard resolution is available exactly when it is least needed.

The failure this produces is not a missed detection but a false one, which is worse. In our node four
independent code paths observe a peer's head: the outbound handshake, the inbound handshake, a head
carried on a peer record, and the gossip round. Each samples at a different instant and nothing
sequences their arrival, so \emph{gossip records size 8, then a reconnect delivers the size-6 head it
captured moments earlier} is ordinary concurrency. Compared pairwise without a proof, it reads as a
tree that shrank. Because a fork is the strongest signal the system has, the peer is demoted to a
state only an operator can lift --- so a node partitions itself from honest peers, during a partition,
on evidence it manufactured. In our deployed four-node testbed one uplink flap produced this against
\textbf{two of three peers at once}, both with the same manufactured reason (\emph{tree shrank from 8
to 6}), and neither log had been rewritten.

\paragraph{A rule that costs no round trip.} Signed tree heads already carry a signed timestamp, so
the readings can be ordered without asking anyone anything. Let $(t, n)$ be the timestamp and size of
an incoming head and $(t_0, n_0)$ the recorded one:

\begin{enumerate}\itemsep1pt
  \item if $t < t_0$ the reading is stale: return \textsc{unknown}, and \textbf{do not record it};
  \item otherwise if $n < n_0$ the tree regressed: report \textsc{forked};
  \item otherwise record $(t, n)$ and report \textsc{ok}.
\end{enumerate}

This does not replace a consistency proof --- it is strictly weaker, and a node that can reach the
peer should still fetch one. What it does is remove the false accusations in the window where no proof
can be obtained, which is the window that matters here.

\paragraph{Clause 1's second half is the part that is easy to get wrong.} The natural implementation
discards the stale reading by overwriting the record with it, or by recording it and comparing next
time. Both reintroduce the bug one step later: writing $(t,n)$ back rolls the ledger to size~6, so a
later honest observation is compared against a value that should never have been stored. The stale
reading must leave no trace.

\paragraph{Genuine rollback is untouched, and we assert both directions.} An attacker must now present
a head that is both newer \emph{and} smaller, which still trips clause 2. The regression test asserts
that a smaller-and-older head leaves trust intact and leaves the recorded size at 8, \emph{and} that a
smaller head with an equal-or-newer timestamp still reports \textsc{forked}. Without the second
assertion the guard would be indistinguishable from deleting the check.

\subsection{Convergence under reordering}

Two nodes may receive the same set of signed envelopes in different orders. Any projection that
folds them in arrival order will therefore diverge. We found this in a vote handler, which merged by
arrival: two nodes receiving the same two signed votes in opposite orders disagreed permanently on
both the stored value and the derived score. The fix is to impose a total order using only fields
inside the signed envelope --- here $(\textit{created\_at\_ms}, \textit{content\_id})$ --- so that
every node folds identically regardless of delivery order. The property is asserted over \emph{every
permutation} of a conflicting set rather than over one arrival order, because a test that fixes the
order cannot distinguish an order-independent fold from a lucky one.

\subsection{Bridging two islands}

At L3, two ISP islands are severed from each other but a node holds transit on both. That node binds
each outbound federation connection to the source address of the corresponding uplink, and relays
between islands only when it has a trusted peer on each side. Crossings are quota-accounted per
uplink pair rather than per peer, so an island with three nodes is not charged three times for one
link's traffic, and bulk traffic draws on a bucket capped at half the pair's grant so that a bulk
backlog cannot starve emergency broadcasts.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Adversary and Scope}
\label{sec:adversary}

We assume an adversary that controls routing within its own autonomous system, can null-route
prefixes, inspect and block transit it carries, throttle selectively, partition the country or one
ISP from another, and operate its own nodes as a legitimate peer. We assume it cannot break Ed25519
or SHA-256, cannot extract keys from an uncompromised device, is not a \emph{global} passive
observer, and does not control a majority of the peer set.

Within that model the design resists: content suppression by a server, since content is valid the
instant its author signs it and withheld approval is removed as a mechanism rather than policed;
tampering by a relay, since signatures cover received bytes and non-canonical encodings are rejected;
undetected log rewriting, via gossiped tree heads; write amplification from invalid floods, via the
no-write prefix of the pipeline; and loss of reach under partition, via scope-aware path selection
and bridging.

The exclusions are numerous and deliberate. There is \textbf{no traffic-analysis resistance} --- no
cover traffic, no mixing, and an observer on both islands can correlate flows --- and \textbf{no
transport metadata protection}: an ISP sees that a node federates, roughly with whom and how much.
\textbf{Endpoint compromise} is out of scope beyond revocation, which only helps once it reaches
peers, which is what the adversary is attacking. Fork detection \textbf{assumes one honest observer}
gossips a conflicting head. There is \textbf{no formal unlinkability proof} between the pseudonymous
and identified identity planes; the separation is architectural. And Sybil resistance is
\textbf{deliberately not derived from identity} --- cost is the anti-abuse primitive, because
requiring identity to stop spam hands the adversary a censorship lever --- so it bounds the rate of
abuse, not the number of identities.

Each exclusion could be bought with availability, or with the ability to run on a single-board
computer over an intermittent link. Where a defence and reach conflict we choose reach, and say so.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

\subsection{Testbed}

Measurements come from container testbeds on one host. The partition testbed runs four nodes across
three networks, two of which are declared internal so that no route exists between them except
through the bridging node (Figure~\ref{fig:testbed}); each island has its own database and cache,
and the islands occupy separate address spaces. The scale testbed generates an $N$-node federation chain, $2 \le N \le 16$,
in which node $i$ is reachable from node $0$ by exactly one path of exactly $i$ hops.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  font=\scriptsize,
  node/.style={draw,rounded corners=1pt,minimum width=7mm,minimum height=4.5mm},
  br/.style={draw,rounded corners=1pt,fill=black!10,minimum width=9mm,minimum height=4.5mm},
  lbl/.style={font=\scriptsize\itshape},
  >={Stealth[length=1.3mm]}
]
% island A
\node[node] (a1) at (0,0.55) {a1};
\node[node] (a2) at (0,-0.55) {a2};
\draw (a1) -- (a2);
\node[lbl,above=0.5mm of a1] {island A \textperiodcentered\ AS 64501};
% bridge
\node[br] (b) at (2.15,0) {bridge};
% island B
\node[node] (b1) at (4.3,0) {b1};
\node[lbl,above=0.5mm of b1] {island B \textperiodcentered\ AS 64502};
\draw (a1) -- (b);
\draw (a2) -- (b);
\draw (b)  -- (b1);
% severed exchange
\draw[dashed] (a1) to[out=35,in=145] node[pos=0.5,above,lbl] {exchange} (b1);
\draw[line width=0.7pt] (2.15,0.83) -- (2.55,1.17);
\draw[line width=0.7pt] (2.55,0.83) -- (2.15,1.17);
\node[lbl,right=0.5mm of b,yshift=-3.5mm] {two source addrs};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The partition testbed. Islands A and B occupy separate address spaces on networks declared
internal, so no route exists between them once the exchange is cut. The bridging node holds transit
on both and binds each outbound connection to the corresponding uplink's source address.}
\label{fig:testbed}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Why one configuration and not a sweep.} We attempted to sweep chain length and drain
interval and discarded the result. One configuration happened to repeat, and it disagreed with
itself: an identical four-node, 500\,ms run measured 1348\,ms at the first hop early in the sweep and
6630\,ms later, with delivery falling to 88\%. Recreating the host returned it to 888\,ms and 100\%:
the varying quantity was host contention, not the parameter. We report one clean configuration rather
than a sweep whose independent variable we could not isolate.

We measure propagation from the timestamp each node records when it stores an envelope, inside the
same transaction as the projection. A hop's latency is therefore a subtraction between two numbers
the system wrote for its own reasons, with no observer in the path. This is valid here only because all
containers share the host clock, so skew is zero by construction; a distributed deployment would need
NTP discipline and an error bar. An earlier figure obtained by polling over HTTP is an upper bound
charged for a poll interval plus a round trip, and was discarded.

\subsection{Cross-implementation agreement}
\label{sec:eval-vectors}

The admission gate of \S\ref{sec:canonical} is only viable if the accepted encoding is genuinely
agreed across implementations; otherwise it becomes a compatibility bug that rejects honest peers.
Three independent implementations of the canonical encoder --- in TypeScript, Rust and Python, written
against the specification rather than ported from one another --- agree byte-for-byte on all 16
conformance vectors, including field omission, NFC normalisation, domain separation and plane
separation cases. This is what makes strictness affordable, and it is the answer to the natural
objection that two implementations will eventually disagree.

\subsection{Correctness under partition}

With the two-node deployment, a post published on one node projects on the other with an identical
content identifier and an origin-derived community identifier, across fully independent datastores.
Two recoverability properties are asserted rather than assumed. Rebuilding every populated collection
from the envelope log alone reproduces it \emph{byte-identically}, so restoring a node needs the log
and nothing else. And the client recomputes signature status and log inclusion on device from the
provenance block, rejecting a receipt signed by a key other than the one it names, a tree head not
covering the content, and a content identifier lifted from a \emph{different} envelope --- the
relay-relabelling attack, refused on the client.
The federation suite covers 31 assertions spanning admission, quota, streaming, resumable backfill,
tree-head gossip with fork detection, and directory exchange.

\subsection{Multi-hop propagation}

Figure~\ref{fig:hops} reports an eight-node chain, 200 paced samples, with a 1000\,ms outbox drain
interval. All 200 envelopes reached hop~7, and the seven-hop median is 4125\,ms against a p99 of
6115\,ms.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=\columnwidth, height=42mm,
  font=\scriptsize,
  xlabel={hop count}, ylabel={latency (ms)},
  xlabel near ticks, ylabel near ticks,
  xmin=0.6, xmax=7.4, ymin=0, ymax=6600,
  xtick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7},
  ytick={0,2000,4000,6000},
  grid=major, grid style={jbMist},
  axis line style={jbInk}, tick label style={text=jbInk},
  legend style={at={(0.02,0.98)},anchor=north west,draw=jbMist,
                font=\scriptsize,row sep=-1pt,inner sep=2pt},
  legend cell align=left
]
\addplot[jbBlue,mark=*,mark size=1.4pt,line width=0.7pt]
  coordinates {(1,613)(2,820)(3,1753)(4,2352)(5,3355)(6,3660)(7,4125)};
\addlegendentry{p50}
\addplot[jbRose,mark=triangle*,mark size=1.8pt,line width=0.7pt,dashed]
  coordinates {(1,1104)(2,2251)(3,3341)(4,3912)(5,4913)(6,5215)(7,6115)};
\addlegendentry{p99}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Propagation latency by hop count. Eight-node chain, 200 paced samples, zero loss: all 200
envelopes reached hop~7. Growth is linear in hop count, and the slope is set by the outbox drain
interval rather than by the protocol.}
\label{fig:hops}
\end{figure}

Per-hop cost is dominated by the drain interval rather than by the protocol. Predicting
$\textit{drain}/2 + \textit{fixed}$ and measuring at two operating points isolates the constant: at a
1000\,ms drain the seven-hop median implies 589\,ms per hop and a fixed residual of 89\,ms; at 250\,ms
it implies 196\,ms per hop and a residual of 71\,ms. The residual is stable at \textbf{71--89\,ms
across a fourfold change in the queueing parameter}, and represents the protocol's real per-hop cost:
verify, project, append to the witness log, re-enqueue. Everything above it is a scheduling choice an
operator makes for battery and bandwidth reasons.

\textbf{We report the offered-load regime with every figure, because it dominates the result.}
Publishing the same 200 envelopes as fast as the origin accepted them produced a first-hop median of
10.0\,s and a 95th percentile of 173\,s, while the \emph{marginal} cost of hops 2--7 remained at
0.4--1.1\,s. That shape is queueing and contention on a shared database, not propagation. Both are
correct measurements of different questions, and averaging them would describe neither.

\subsection{Bridged crossing}

Crossing an ISP boundary is not one envelope. A newly-published post that a receiving island can
render depends on the author's certificate and the community record, so three envelopes must cross
and project in dependency order --- a distinction that matters, because measuring only the post
conceals where the time goes (\S\ref{sec:lessons}).

With the exchange severed and the bridge carrying all traffic between islands, the three envelopes
arrive on the far island 0.5\,s, 1.2\,s and 2.1\,s after publication. The comparable figures with
both islands healthy are 1.5\,s, 1.5\,s and 1.9\,s, so \textbf{cutting the exchange does not
meaningfully degrade crossing latency} once traffic is routed through the bridge. The container
partition gate passes all 19 criteria.

Inside the bridging container, \texttt{/proc/net/tcp} shows established federation sockets leaving
from \emph{both} configured source addresses, which is the assertion no in-process harness can make.
Per-direction accounting confirms the crossing is attributed to the correct uplink pair, and the
reserved-capacity check confirms bulk traffic drew on a bucket capped at half the pair grant.

\subsection{An invalid-envelope flood does not become write load}
\label{sec:flood}

Section~\ref{sec:design} claims that because steps 1--12 perform no database writes, a flood of
invalid envelopes costs CPU but cannot amplify into write load. That is a claim about a denial-of-service
surface, so we measured it rather than asserting it.

Random bytes are a weak test: they die at parse in microseconds. The costly case is a
\emph{well-formed} envelope with a wrong signature, which must be parsed, checked for canonicality and
verified before it can be refused. We took a genuine envelope from the node's own log and mutated one
byte inside the signed region --- invalidating the signature and changing the content identifier, so
every request is novel and none is short-circuited by deduplication.

Against a single node, 3000 such envelopes at concurrency~16 were absorbed at \textbf{730\,req/s}, and
the node's database recorded \textbf{zero writes}: the envelope log was unchanged, and a
collection-level profiler --- which captured read operations over the same window, confirming it was
live --- logged no insert, update or delete. Resident memory fell from 86\,MiB to 61\,MiB as the
garbage collector caught up, and CPU returned to idle immediately.

The rejection histogram is more interesting than the throughput, and we report it because it changes
what the number means. Only 9\% of the flood reached signature verification; \textbf{90\% was shed by
the per-peer rate limiter first}, before any asymmetric cryptography was performed. The no-write
property is therefore real but is the \emph{second} line of defence, and a flood measurement that did
not report which step did the rejecting would overstate what the pipeline ordering contributes.

\paragraph{The measurement also found a defect, which is why it was worth doing.} Eight of the 3000
responses were HTTP 500 rather than a typed rejection. The write endpoint decoded untrusted bytes
\emph{outside} the pipeline in order to label a metric, and a raw decoder exception is not a typed
rejection, so it escaped the error branch --- emitting a stack trace per request and letting the caller
choose how fast the node filled its own disk. The same bytes arriving over federation were refused
politely, so the two ingress paths disagreed about what malformed meant. Decoding for a label now
falls back to a constant, and a regression test drives four adversarial encodings that each returned
500 before the change. Fixing it also raised the flood-rejection rate from 440 to 730\,req/s, because
the node had been spending the difference constructing stack traces --- the error path was itself the
amplifier.

\subsection{Read path and footprint}

With a keep-alive client in a single process, the read path sustains 228\,req/s at concurrency~1
(p50 4.1\,ms), 750\,req/s at concurrency~8 (p50 9.4\,ms) and 688\,req/s at concurrency~32 (p50
44.2\,ms). We note that measuring this with one process per request instead reported 144\,ms, which
was timing process creation rather than the server.

An idle federating node holds \textbf{62\,MiB} resident, rising to \textbf{233\,MiB} at 54\% CPU
under a sustained bulk crossing. With the database (147\,MiB) and cache (3.7\,MiB) co-resident the
total is roughly \textbf{384\,MiB} against a 512\,MB single-board-computer target. That is
\emph{tight rather than comfortable}, and the database dominates it --- a deployment on constrained
hardware should expect storage, not the node, to be the binding constraint.

\subsection{Encoded size against a measured ActivityPub baseline}

Encoded envelopes, including a 64-byte signature, are 155\,B for a check-in, 220\,B for a forum post
and 243\,B for a Bangla-language broadcast. To make this a comparison rather than an isolated fact we
measured \textbf{80 real \texttt{Create}/\texttt{Note} activities} from two independent stock
ActivityPub instances, taken from actor outboxes so they are as the servers emit them. Because a
collection hoists the JSON-LD context to the collection while an activity delivered to a remote inbox
carries its own, we re-attached the context each host actually serves on a standalone object.

