\label{fig:size}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:size} and Table~\ref{tab:size} report overhead --- encoded bytes minus authored text --- because content
length is a confound and overhead is what a protocol designer controls. On that measure the ratio is
\textbf{19.5$\times$--25.9$\times$}. The JSON-LD context declaration alone, identical on both hosts at
927\,B, exceeds our entire signed forum post by a factor of 4.2.

\textbf{Two qualifications keep this honest.} First, compression removes most of the advantage: the
ActivityPub payload gzips to a 1074\,B median, whereas our envelopes do \emph{not} compress --- a
random 64-byte signature is incompressible and gzip makes all three of ours larger (220\,B becomes
243\,B). The compressed comparison is therefore \textbf{4.4$\times$--6.9$\times$}, and we quote both.
Second, we charge ActivityPub nothing for authentication, although our figures include a signature
that makes the envelope verifiable at rest; deployed ActivityPub authenticates with a transport-level
HTTP Signature header that no third party can check against a stored object. A structurally fair
comparison would widen the gap, not narrow it. We also measure no HTTP framing on either side, and
count one activity where real delivery is per-follower fanout.

This measures per-message cost on a constrained link and nothing else: JSON-LD buys extensibility
and semantic interoperation a fixed schema does not, and we measure none of it.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Comparison with Related Systems}
\label{sec:comparison}

No quantitative baseline exists for "operates across an ISP partition", so we compare capabilities.
Table~\ref{tab:matrix} scores prior systems on the ladder and on the integrity properties this design
turns on. Every cell was checked against the system's own specification.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Capability comparison. \CIRCLE~provided and specified, \LEFTcircle~partial, optional or
non-normative, \Circle~not provided, $\ast$~implemented here but never observed end to end
(\S\ref{sec:limitations}). \textsc{nf}~indicates the specification is silent. \emph{No inbound} means
a node with no reachable address still federates in both directions (\S\ref{sec:cgnat}).}
\label{tab:matrix}
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.2pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
 & L0--L3 & L4--L5 & no in- & signed & verify & no re- & log & tomb- \\
 & span & span & bound & at rest & w/o srv & encode & gossip & stone \\
\midrule
DTN / BPv7        & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE & \LEFTcircle & \Circle & \textsc{nf} & \textsc{nf} & --- \\
Serval (Rhizome)  & \LEFTcircle & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE & \textsc{nf} & \textsc{nf} & \Circle \\
Bridgefy          & \Circle     & \LEFTcircle & \CIRCLE & \Circle     & \Circle & ---         & \Circle     & --- \\
ActivityPub       & \LEFTcircle & \Circle     & \Circle & \LEFTcircle & \Circle & \Circle     & \Circle     & \LEFTcircle \\
Matrix            & \LEFTcircle & \Circle     & \Circle & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE & \Circle     & \Circle     & \CIRCLE \\
AT Protocol       & \Circle     & \Circle     & \Circle & \CIRCLE     & \Circle & \CIRCLE     & \Circle     & \Circle \\
\textbf{This work}& \CIRCLE     & $\ast$      & \CIRCLE & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE     & \CIRCLE \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Four entries cut against us. \textbf{DTN spans the whole ladder} and more cheaply, being designed for
exactly that; what it lacks is everything above the bearer. \textbf{DTN and Serval both beat us on the
lower rungs}, where our entry is $\ast$ --- we have code, they have deployments. \textbf{Serval
verifies without a server more simply}: its bundle identifier \emph{is} the key, where we resolve a
certificate. And \textbf{Matrix's redaction preserves more than is commonly assumed} --- the event
stays in the graph with its identifier, sender, timestamp, parent links, hashes and signatures.

The \emph{no inbound} column is where the federated systems separate from the rest. ActivityPub,
Matrix and the AT Protocol all deliver by pushing to an address the receiver publishes, so a receiver
behind carrier-grade NAT cannot participate without a third party holding an address for it. The
store-and-forward systems score \CIRCLE\ here for the same reason we do: a property we share with
DTN and Serval, not one we invented.

Two entries are frequently misread in the other direction. ActivityPub scores \LEFTcircle\ on
tombstoning because Activity Streams 2.0~\cite{activitystreams} does define a \texttt{Tombstone} type and the dominant
implementation does keep a table by that name --- but nothing serves it, no such object is emitted,
and its only reader is a replay guard. The AT Protocol scores \Circle\ on verification without a
server because although its commits are signed, neither the signature nor the commit indicates the
key type or the public key used; that must be fetched from an external identity directory, which a
shutdown removes. It scores \Circle\ on tombstoning by its own explicit statement that record
deletion leaves no trace of previous contents~\cite{atproto}.

No column in Table~\ref{tab:matrix} is novel on its own. What no prior system holds is all six
simultaneously \emph{while spanning L0--L3}, and the engineering cost of that combination --- not its
novelty --- is what this paper reports.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{The lower rungs are unobserved.} L4 and L5 are implemented and unit-tested but never
exercised end to end: no phone-to-phone mesh between real handsets (the available devices were on
different subnets, so discovery could not see across them), no radio stack running on a device, no
physical drill. These are marked $\ast$ in Table~\ref{tab:matrix} and no claim here depends on them.

\textbf{Everything is measured in containers on one host, and that host is a confound.} Clock skew is
zero by construction, the network is a virtual bridge, and one storage engine is shared by several
nodes. This is not merely a caveat: as \S\ref{sec:eval} reports, repeating one configuration late in a
sweep changed its first-hop median by a factor of five, so this testbed cannot separate a parameter
effect from its own contention beyond a single clean run. Chain length and queueing parameter
therefore remain unswept, and no wide-area, lossy-link or real-radio measurement exists.

\textbf{Unlinkability is architectural, not proven.} The separation between identity planes has no
formal argument and no anonymity-set analysis.

\textbf{Single-operator testbed.} Every node was configured by us, so the trust dynamics ---
probationary admission, vouching, demotion --- are unobserved under independent operators, which is
where they matter.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Lessons}
\label{sec:lessons}

\paragraph{Bugs have a minimum topology.} The identifier defect of \S\ref{sec:federation} needed two
nodes, and the delivery stall below needed three, one of them unreachable while the others stayed up.
Neither suite was weak; the topologies were. Relatedly, our in-process partition suite passed all 22
assertions while the container gate failed --- the assertions that earned their place are the ones no
in-process harness can make, such as reading \texttt{/proc/net/tcp} to confirm which source address a
socket actually left from.

\paragraph{An overdue queue entry with zero attempts is a different failure from a failing one.}
This is the finding we would most want another team to take away. The bridged crossing of
\S\ref{sec:eval} cost 407.6\,s after the cut, and the standing explanation was that it was merely
slow. It was not: every outbound row sat at \emph{zero attempts} while overdue by 150 seconds.
Retries that fail tell you about the destination; work never attempted tells you about the scheduler.

The cause was a drain pass that delivered to peers serially with no deadline. A severed link
blackholes rather than refuses, so the attempt to the unreachable peer neither succeeded nor errored
and every peer behind it waited --- including the bridge, whose purpose is to route around the
partition that caused the stall. The node degraded to the availability of its \emph{worst} peer
instead of its best, inverting what multi-homing buys. Draining peers concurrently under a deadline
returned the crossing to 2.1\,s and the gate to 19 of 19.

%% ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusion}

Availability under partition is a design ordering, not a feature. Holding it first forces specific
and sometimes uncomfortable choices: identifiers derived from signed bytes rather than from the node
that stores them, admission cost charged once at the origin rather than re-verified on receipt,
canonical encoding used to admit rather than to verify, and a rejection where a more forgiving system
would repair. We have shown that this composition operates from global reach down to bridged ISP
islands, measured what it costs in latency, memory and bytes, and reported a partition-availability
defect that only a deployed multi-peer testbed could expose. The rungs below the ISP island remain
implemented and unobserved, and closing that gap on real devices and real radios is the work we think
matters next.

